// Question: Explain the JPA entity lifecycle and dirty checking.
// What are the four entity states, and what transitions exist between them?
// Why does this code work without calling save()?
// @Transactional
// public void updateEmail(Long userId, String email) {
//     User user = userRepo.findById(userId).get(); // returns MANAGED entity
//     user.setEmail(email); // just a setter!
//     // NO save() call — yet the database IS updated. Why?
// }
// Answer:

solution.code:
// JPA Entity Lifecycle States:
//
// 1. NEW (Transient)
//    - Created with 'new', not yet associated with persistence context
//    - Has no ID (or ID not yet known to JPA)
//    - Not in database, not tracked
//    - Example: User user = new User(); // NEW state
//
// 2. MANAGED (Persistent)
//    - Associated with an open EntityManager/persistence context
//    - Has an ID, Hibernate is watching it
//    - ANY change to the object's fields WILL be persisted at flush time
//    - How to enter: entityManager.persist(entity) OR entityManager.find()
//      OR loaded via repository.findById() within a @Transactional method
//
// 3. DETACHED
//    - Previously managed, but the persistence context closed
//    - Still has an ID, but changes are NOT automatically persisted
//    - How to enter: transaction ends, session.close(), entityManager.detach()
//    - To re-attach: entityManager.merge(entity) → returns new MANAGED copy
//
// 4. REMOVED
//    - Scheduled for deletion at next flush
//    - entityManager.remove(entity) or repository.delete(entity)
//
// HOW DIRTY CHECKING WORKS:
// When Hibernate loads an entity, it stores a SNAPSHOT (deep copy) of the
// original state. At flush time (before commit, or explicitly), Hibernate
// compares every MANAGED entity to its snapshot.
// If any field differs → it generates an UPDATE SQL automatically.
//
// That's why setEmail() alone is enough:
// 1. @Transactional starts → persistence context opens
// 2. findById() loads User → entity is MANAGED, snapshot stored
// 3. setEmail() modifies the managed object
// 4. @Transactional ends → flush is triggered
// 5. Hibernate compares User to its snapshot → email field differs
// 6. Hibernate generates: UPDATE users SET email=? WHERE id=?
// 7. Transaction commits
//
// This is also why forgetting @Transactional causes bugs:
// findById() runs its own tiny transaction, closes it → entity is DETACHED
// setEmail() modifies a DETACHED entity → NO UPDATE is issued

