// Question: Explain Hibernate's first and second level caches.
// When is each active? What are the guarantees and trade-offs?
// Answer:

solution.code:
// FIRST-LEVEL CACHE (L1) — Always Active
//
// - Scope: EntityManager / Hibernate Session = one transaction (usually)
// - Automatically used with no configuration needed
// - Guarantee: within a single transaction, if you load entity with ID=5 twice,
//   Hibernate executes SELECT only ONCE. Second call returns cached object.
// - Identity guarantee: same ID = same object reference within the session
// - Cleared when: transaction ends, EntityManager closes, or
//   entityManager.clear() is called
// - Cannot be disabled
//
// Example:
// @Transactional
// void example() {
//     User u1 = userRepo.findById(1L).get();  // SELECT issued
//     User u2 = userRepo.findById(1L).get();  // NO SELECT — returns u1 from L1 cache
//     assert u1 == u2;  // same object reference!
// }

// SECOND-LEVEL CACHE (L2) — Optional, Opt-in
//
// - Scope: EntityManagerFactory = shared across all sessions/transactions
// - Must be explicitly enabled: @Cache on entity class + L2 provider (Ehcache, Caffeine)
// - Survives transaction boundaries — cached between requests
// - Use cases: read-heavy, rarely-changing reference data (countries, currencies,
//   product catalog, configuration tables)
// - CacheConcurrencyStrategy choices:
//   READ_ONLY       → immutable reference data (fastest)
//   NONSTRICT_READ_WRITE → occasional updates, eventual consistency ok
//   READ_WRITE      → strict consistency, row-level locking in cache
//   TRANSACTIONAL   → full tx isolation, requires JTA
//
// RISKS OF L2 CACHE:
// 1. Native SQL or JDBC updates bypass Hibernate → L2 cache has STALE data
//    Fix: evict affected regions manually or avoid native writes to cached entities
// 2. In clustered deployments: nodes have separate L2 caches (unless clustered cache)
//    → Node A updates, Node B serves stale cached data
//    Fix: use distributed L2 cache (Infinispan, Hazelcast) or disable L2 for writes
// 3. Incorrect CacheConcurrencyStrategy → dirty reads, lost updates
//
// Rule: L2 cache is worth the complexity only for tables that are:
// - Read FAR more often than written (read/write ratio > 10:1)
// - Not mutated by external processes (no raw SQL pipelines)
// - Small to medium in size (large tables = large cache = memory pressure)

