// Question: What is the persistence context and its scope in Spring?
// Explain what happens to entities across transaction boundaries.
// What common bugs does misunderstanding this cause?
// Answer:

solution.code:
// PERSISTENCE CONTEXT:
// An in-memory cache that maps entity IDs to managed entity objects.
// Also called 'Session' in Hibernate terminology.
// It is the mechanism behind:
// - Entity identity (same ID = same object within context)
// - Dirty checking (tracks changes for automatic SQL generation)
// - First-level cache (avoids redundant SELECTs within a session)

// DEFAULT SCOPE IN SPRING: Transaction-Scoped
// Each @Transactional method gets its own persistence context.
// When the transaction ends, the context closes and all entities become DETACHED.

// LIFECYCLE:
// @Transactional method starts
//   → EntityManager created (persistence context opens)
//   → Entities loaded via repository are MANAGED
//   → Changes tracked automatically
// @Transactional method ends
//   → Flush (pending SQL sent to DB)
//   → Commit (transaction completes)
//   → EntityManager destroyed (persistence context closes)
//   → All previously managed entities are now DETACHED

// COMMON BUG 1: LazyInitializationException
// @RestController
// @GetMapping
// public Order getOrder(Long id) {
//     Order order = orderService.findById(id);  // tx ends, context closes
//     order.getItems().size(); // DETACHED — LazyInitializationException!
//     return order;
// }
// Fix: @Transactional on the service method OR JOIN FETCH OR return DTO

// COMMON BUG 2: Lost Update (Modifying Detached Entity)
// Order order = orderRepo.findById(1L).get();  // tx ends → DETACHED
// order.setStatus(SHIPPED);  // modifying a DETACHED entity
// // No @Transactional, no .save() → the status change is LOST
// // Fix: wrap in @Transactional, OR call orderRepo.save(order)

// COMMON BUG 3: Unintended Cascade via Dirty Checking
// @Transactional
// void method() {
//     List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findAll(); // all loaded MANAGED
//     orders.get(0).setStatus(CANCELLED); // dirty!
//     // Even without .save(), this WILL generate an UPDATE at flush time
//     // This surprises developers who think 'I didn't call save'
// }

// EXTENDED PERSISTENCE CONTEXT (not default in Spring)
// EntityManager can span multiple transactions (used in stateful EJBs, rare in Spring)
// Spring Boot defaults to transaction-scoped; use @PersistenceContext(type=EXTENDED)
// to opt in — but be very careful with memory and stale data.
